BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://events.yorku.ca/events/featured-collection-in-york-university-libraries-on-complexities-facing-people-experiencing-homelessness/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:Featured Collection in York University Libraries on complexities f
 acing people experiencing homelessness
DTSTART:20261005T040000Z
DTEND:20261020T040000Z
DTSTAMP:20261001T163700Z
UID:MEC-3f6d017d3e728b057bcc082a7db75a57@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261005T040000Z
SEQUENCE:5
CATEGORIES:Libraries,Teaching &amp\; Learning,Well-being Week
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261001
DESCRIPTION:In recognition of World Homeless Day on Oct. 10\, Complex Comm
 unities and York University Libraries have partnered to present a featured
  collection from Oct. 5 to 15 highlighting the complexities of homelessnes
 s and fostering greater awareness and understanding of the experiences of 
 those affected. \nWorld Homeless Day\, observed annually on Oct. 10\, rai
 ses awareness of the experiences and needs of people facing homelessness\,
  promotes local solutions\, and encourages community action around the wor
 ld. This featured collection highlights print and electronic materials fro
 m the library’s collections on homelessness\, mental health and well-bei
 ng to foster greater understanding of these critical issues and support te
 aching\, learning\, and research in these areas. We invite you to browse t
 he book display on the first floor of Scott Library\, along with our virtu
 al display. \nIn addition to the books and resources on display\, this f
 eatured collection will showcase a recent Photovoice project developed by 
 TNG Community Services. Photovoice is a qualitative research method that u
 ses participant-generated photographs and personal narratives to transform
  lived experiences into actionable knowledge. TNG’s project\, What Does
  24 Hours Look Like When You’re Experiencing Homelessness?\, reflects 
 the organization’s commitment to addressing the stigma and discriminatio
 n faced by people experiencing homelessness and amplifying their voices th
 rough storytelling and research. \nIt’s easy to assume the answer is d
 ark and grim and that the only light at the end of the tunnel is daybreak\
 , a hot cup of coffee\, or a shower at the drop-in. Scary\, stressful\, lo
 nely\, dangerous: all apt words to describe the realities of being margina
 lly or precariously housed. But they are also limiting descriptors\, ones 
 that flatten people into stereotypes and leave little room for curiosity\,
  complexity\, or empathy. \nStigma is everywhere\, and few experience it 
 more acutely than those living outside traditional social norms. \nOver t
 he course of three weeks\, seven participants from TNG Community Services
 ’ Urban Health and Homelessness Services drop-in used 35mm disposable ca
 meras to document their daily lives through photography. The project invit
 ed participants to capture the mundane\, the joyful\, the gritty and the d
 eeply personal\; moments that assert presence\, permanence\, and the unden
 iable right to take up space. \nPhotography became both an act of creativ
 ity and an act of resistance. \nA leader in the social services sector in
  Toronto\, The Neighbourhood Group helps more than 45\,000 low-income pe
 ople and families in 30 locations across Toronto. With more than 1\,000 st
 aff and the support of over 900 volunteers\, our free programs and service
 s address the most pressing issues in our community: poverty\, homelessnes
 s\, mental health\, unemployment\, social isolation\, substance abuse\, co
 nflict resolution\, violence\, youth alienation\, and the settlement of ne
 wcomers.  \nThis project was important to me because others have intere
 st to see what I see. I grew up in the system. I only have my daughter
  and my other ones don’t talk to me and everyone is scarce. That you
  guys to show interest in me is great. I hope others seeing these pictur
 es will have empathy and humbleness. I’d like people to put my shoes 
 on for 24 hours\, let’s see how hard it is in each other’s lives\, 
 so we can respect one another.  – TNGCS Photovoice participant \nOur h
 eartfelt thanks to TNG Community Services and its community for sharing th
 is powerful Photovoice project and amplifying the voices and experiences o
 f people facing homelessness. \nA special thank you to York University Li
 braries and especially to the Collections Engagement Group\, for their wor
 k in bringing this featured collection to life. \nFor more information ab
 out Complex Communities\, reach out!  ComplexC@yorku.ca\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261001
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/featured-collection-in-york-university-
 libraries-on-complexities-facing-people-experiencing-homelessness/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
