SUMMARY:Writing\, Editing and Publishing 'The Bloodwood Society'
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T200000
UID:69ce9c85e2a2952aed0c3b27@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:​Everyone has a story to tell\, and there are more ways than
  ever to share your story with the world. But how do you go about collecti
 ng your ideas into a novel? How do you edit it properly so your readers wi
 ll have the best possible experience with it and how do you get your story
  into their hands once your novel is done?\nThis workshop will talk about 
 the creative writing process\, the editing experience\, and the step you'l
 l take to self-publish your first novel. Ainsley Doty shares and talks abo
 ut her creative process and experience writing and self-publishing her fir
 st novel\, The Bloodwood Society.
LOCATION:Malvern\, Community Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ce9c85e2a2952aed0c3b27
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://vpepc.apps01.yorku.ca/machform/view.php?id=32229"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:What Can the University Be?
DTSTART:20261006T183000Z
DTEND:20261006T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260929T171100Z
UID:MEC-d9466601bb1cec0a0df354707b5a1c08@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261006T183000Z
SEQUENCE:3
CATEGORIES:Well-being,Well-being Week
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20260929
DESCRIPTION:A Conversation on Academic Freedom\, Freedom of Expression\, a
 nd the Future of Higher Education\nUniversities are being asked to do many
  things at once: defend free inquiry\, navigate competing convictions\, su
 pport community well-being\, and demonstrate public value. Join us for a t
 imely discussion on the role of the university as a public institution and
  the conditions that make meaningful dialogue across difference possible.\
 nKeynote Address\nShannon Dea\, Professor of Philosophy\, Saint Mary’s U
 niversity\n“The Sheeran Effect? Institutional Neutrality\, Scholarly Com
 mitment\, and the Academic Mission”\nRespondent\nPaul Axelrod\, Professo
 r Emeritus\, Faculty of Education\, York University\nDrawing on their rich
  scholarship and depth of expertise\, speakers will situate current tensio
 ns and opportunities around academic freedom\, freedom of expression\, aca
 demic community\, care\, and civility.\nJoin the Conversation\nJoin us for
  an engaging dialogue exploring the university as a vital public instituti
 on and the conditions needed for intellectual openness\, productive disagr
 eement\, and meaningful exchange across differences while sustaining commu
 nities of learning.\n\nDate: October 6\, 2026 \nTime: 2:30PM-4PM \nLocatio
 n: 519 Kaneff Tower\n\nRegistration Link\nLight refreshments will be serve
 d.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20260929
LOCATION:519 Kaneff Tower
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://vpepc.apps01.yorku.ca/machform/view.php?id=32229
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
