SUMMARY:Sociology Indigenous Lecture Series: The Worst Trickster Story Eve
 r Told: Understanding American Law Through an Indigenous Lens 
DTSTART:20261007T183000Z
DTEND:20261007T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T194000Z
UID:MEC-d9f9c4243f3556c146d09809739084bf@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261007T183000Z
SEQUENCE:20
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://events.yorku.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/
 08/Calendar.jpg
CATEGORIES:Advancement/Alumni,LA&amp\;PS Department of Sociology
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20260821
DESCRIPTION:Join us as guest speaker professor Keith Richotte Jr. 
 examines how the U.S. doctrine of plenary power—granting sweeping author
 ity over Native American nations—came to be understood as constitutional
 \, despite its origins in deeply flawed and outdated assumptions. Through 
 a distinctive blend of legal analysis and Native history\, Richotte\, Jr
 . explores when and why this transformation occurred\, and what it revea
 ls about constitutional interpretation today. Centring Indigenous perspect
 ives and the tradition of trickster stories\, the talk invites audiences t
 o rethink this history and imagine a more just\, equitable and c
 onstitutional future.  \nRegister Now\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821
LOCATION:802 South Ross Building @ 4700 Keele Street\, Toronto ON\, M3J 1P
 3
ORGANIZER;CN="Department of Sociology":MAILTO:sociadmin@yorku.ca
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/sociology-indigenous-lecture-series/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
