BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-ID:gcal_4h7dspldpuviiv4o1slj9o5p5c
X-SOURCE:CITA Seminars
DTSTART:20261008T163300Z
DTEND:20261008T173300Z
DTSTAMP:20261003T050432Z
UID:quf4ll1hclu0iio3l0tmvg2fjs@google.com
CREATED:20260930T183548Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>Title: <b>Nonlinear Dynamical Tides in Highly Eccentric Bina
 ries: Application to Repeating TDEs and Hot Jupiters</b></p>\n\n<p>Abstract
 : Dynamical tides exhibit chaotic growth within highly eccentric binaries (
 >~0.9) to reach a significant energy comparable to the binding energy. As a
  result\, the critical separation for mass transfer\, known as the Roche li
 mit\, can be significantly modified from the classical results based on equ
 ilibrium tides. Using an affine approximation\, we analytically derive the 
 nonlinear tidal coupling coefficients up to the four-mode perturbation leve
 l and apply them into the tidal evolution of a binary. This allows us to ex
 plore the onset of tidal disruption from first-principles hydrodynamical ca
 lculations. In this talk\, I will discuss the implementation of this framew
 ork in two distinct systems: (1) repeating tidal disruption events (TDEs) n
 ear massive black holes\, (2) high-eccentricity migration of Hot Jupiters. 
 In (1)\, we propose a new mechanism to generate repeating bursts from a mas
 sive black hole-star binary at irregular time intervals via accumulation of
  tidal energy across multiple orbits\, which we call “diffusive-tide disrup
 tions”\, in contrast to the common “prompt disruption” scenario where the d
 isruption occurs at every pericenter passage. The mechanism offers a natura
 l explanation of the burst time fluctuations in J0651 that previous models 
 struggle to reproduce. In (2)\, we study the tidal evolution of a migrating
  gaseous planet\, focusing on the suppression of tidal energy growth\, henc
 e mass loss\, due to nonlinear damping. We show that it opens up the high e
 ccentricity region of the parameter space for a planet to complete the migr
 ation to become a Hot Jupiter without being tidally disrupted. The mechanis
 m can potentially resolve the issue of underpredicting the formation rate o
 f Hot Jupiters in earlier work.\n\nOpen to all</p>\n<p>Host: <b>Norman Murr
 ay</b></p>
LAST-MODIFIED:20260930T191537Z
LOCATION:CITA Bridge
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Vincent Lao (University of Montana)
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
