BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-ID:gcal_4h7dspldpuviiv4o1slj9o5p5c
X-SOURCE:CITA Seminars
DTSTART:20261008T180000Z
DTEND:20261008T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20261003T050432Z
UID:03dvqr3pmpvhtjo0j1tkphkdb0@google.com
CREATED:20260729T135301Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>Title: <b>Reading Between the Rings: Protoplanetary Dust Rin
 gs as Probes and Sites of Planet Formation</b></p>\n\n<p>Abstract: The dust
  rings in protoplanetary disks are generally thought to be formed within th
 e pressure bumps that are created by forming planets that reside in the res
 ulting gaps.  Hence\, ring properties should reflect the planet’s mass.  At
  the same time\, ALMA observations have shown that these rings harbor many 
 tens of Earth masses of dust.  Do rings also give rise to planet formation?
   In this talk I present recent work on both aspects of this intriguing que
 stion.  Using 2D hydro (FARGO) simulations of planet-disk interaction\, we 
 show that the ring’s dust mass\, peak location\, and width correlate with p
 lanet mass.  In particular\, the standoff distance between the ring peak an
 d the planet’s orbital radius depends on the Hill radius of the planet – an
 d hence its mass.  We apply these results to predict the masses of planets 
 in well observed disk systems – PDS 70 and WISPIT2.   I then turn to our wo
 rk on dust agglomeration and concentration in generalized Gaussian pressure
  bumps characterized by an amplitude and width (A\,w). We  address the ques
 tion of what conditions are necessary and sufficient to trigger planetesima
 l and planet formation to be in such pressure bumps by sufficient dust conc
 entrations.  We use the DustPy dust- gas evolution code coupled to the N-bo
 dy SyMBAp to explore two different disk models characterized by the Stokes 
 number (the aerodynamic size of a grain) at which grains fragment by collis
 ions\, across a wide range of values of (A\,w).  We find that the amplitude
  A is the primary factor governing both the mass of the single most massive
  planet and the total planetary mass assembled within the first generation 
 formed in the primary pressure bump.  Finally\, we explore the conditions b
 y which planets formed in the primary bump can excite gaps and pressure bum
 ps at larger radii sufficient to host 2nd and 3rd generation planets.</p>\n
 <p>Host: <b>Shantanu Basu</b></p>
LAST-MODIFIED:20261001T144415Z
LOCATION:MP1318A
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Ralph Pudritz (McMaster University)
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
