SUMMARY: The Blackhurst Project: Inhabitants of the Deep
DESCRIPTION:The Blackhurst Project\, a new monthly literary series from B
 lackhurst Cultural Centre\, launches with Jonathan Howard in conversation
  with I. Augustus Durham.\n\nJonathan Howard\, Assistant Professor of Eng
 lish and Black Studies at Yale University and author of Inhabitants of th
 e Deep: The Blueness of Blackness\, joins I. Augustus Durham\, Assistant 
 Professor of English at the University of Toronto and author of Stay Blac
 k and Die: On Melancholy and Genius\, for an evening of Black literature\
 , scholarship and conversation.\n\nIn Inhabitants of the Deep\, Howard un
 dertakes a Black ecocritical study of the “deep”\, encompassing oceans\, 
 rivers\, lakes and the notion of depth itself\, and its place within Afri
 can American literature and expressive culture. Drawing on figures rangin
 g from Olaudah Equiano and Herman Melville to Otis Redding and August Wil
 son\, Howard traces Black relationships to water\, ecology and the histor
 ies of the Middle Passage\, ultimately considering the deep as a site not
  only of displacement and exclusion\, but of Black ecological life.\n\nJo
 in Howard and Durham for a thought-provoking conversation exploring Black
  literature\, history\, culture and intellectual life.\n\nThursday\, Octo
 ber 8\, 2026 | 6:00–8:00 p.m.\nBlackhurst Cultural Centre | 777–779 Bathu
 rst Street\, Toronto\n\nAdmission is free. Registration is required.\n\nM
 ore Info: eventbrite https://www.eventbrite.ca/e/2002569660763
CATEGORIES:Lecture/Book-Talk,In-Conversation,Discussion,Blackhurst-Cultur
 al-Centre
