X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardinermuseum": "https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/after-the-island-artist-panel/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardinermuseum
X-SOURCE:Gardiner Museum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261009T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261009T200000
DTSTAMP:20261002T180604Z
CREATED:20260908T213009Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261002T180604Z
UID:147119-1791568800-1791576000@www.gardinermuseum.on.ca
SUMMARY:After the Island: Artist Panel
DESCRIPTION:After the Island: Artist Panel            \n                                    \n                                \n                        \n                                                \n                                                        Get Tickets                        \n                                            \n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n			\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Friday October 9\, 2026\n6:00 pm – 8:00 pm\nDoors at 5:30 pm\, Panel at 6:00 pm\nExhibition open until 8:00 pm \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Join us for an evening of art and conversation in celebration of the opening of After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics. The panel will feature exhibiting artists PJ Anderson\, Eddy Firmin\, Manuel Mathieu\, and Sharon Norwood\, moderated by Joséphine Denis\, Co-Director and Director of Curatorial Initiatives at the Black Artists’ Networks in Dialogue (BAND) and Dr. Sequoia Miller\, Chief Curator and Deputy Director at the Gardiner Museum. \nThe discussion will be followed by a Q&A and time to explore the exhibition. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n			\n\n        \n          \n          \n                        \n               \n                Gardiner Friends : $15\n                                        \n                        \n               \n                General : $18\n                                        \n                      \n\n           \n            \n              Gardiner Friends\, log in to access your discount.* \nNot a Gardiner Friend yet? Join now at the Friend level or above to save . \n*Discounts on programs and classes do not apply to ‘Annual Pass’ level. \n            \n                    \n          \n            \n  \n            \n                \n                                    Add to cart\n\n                    \n\n\n                \n                            \n           \n                \n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                \n          \n          \n        \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    About the Artists\n                    \n                \n                \n                    PJ Anderson\n                    \n                \n                \n                    PJ Anderson is Jamaican Canadian /  Red River Métis Artist from Thompson\, Manitoba\, Canada. She is a recent MFA Graduate from the University of Manitoba and currently serves as Director of Programming 2024-2027 for the National Council on Education for the Ceramic Arts (NCECA). Anderson’s practice references everyday weaponizations and the systems that reinforce them. deconstructing threat narratives and highlighting the control of visceral fear. \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    \n                    \n                \n                \n                    Eddy Firmin\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Born in Guadeloupe\, Eddy Firmin is an artist-researcher\, lecturer\, curator and teacher who lives and works between Montreal and Halifax. He holds a PhD in Art Studies and Practices from the Université du Québec à Montréal and a Master’s degree from the École supérieure d’art et de design du Havre-Rouen (France). Firmin’s practice questions the transcultural logics of his identity and issues of power\, while also seeking to decolonize the imagination in art. \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    \n                    \n                \n                \n                    Manuel Mathieu\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Manuel Mathieu is a Haitian multidisciplinary artist based in Montreal working across painting\, ceramics\, olfaction\, film\, and installation. He holds an MFA from Goldsmiths\, University of London. His practice investigates historical violence\, erasure\, and spiritual legacy through embodied relationships to memory\, land\, and the natural world. Mathieu’s artistic journey is deeply intertwined with his community. He has initiated and participated in various social and educational projects that use art as a tool for dialogue\, care\, and transmission\, particularly with youth and underrepresented communities. \n \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    \n                    \n                \n                \n                    Sharon Norwood\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Sharon Norwood is a Jamaican-born interdisciplinary artist whose practice spans ceramics\, drawing\, found objects\, and installation. She holds a BFA from the University of South Florida and an MFA in Studio Art from Florida State University. Through material exploration and process-based experimentation\, her work engages with race\, labour\, identity\, and historical narratives\, often examining how systems of power shape cultural memory and ways of seeing. A recurring motif in her practice is the curly line\, which functions both as a formal gesture and a conceptual reference to Afro-textured hair and the Black body. \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Exhibition Partners\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Presenting Sponsors\n                \n                                \n                            \n                    \n                Supporting Partners\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Government Support\n                \n                                \n                                \n                            \n                    \n                Project Partner\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Media Partners\n                \n                                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Thanks To\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/after-the-island-artist-panel/
LOCATION:Gardiner Museum\, 111 Queen's Park\, Toronto\, ON\, M5S 2C7\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Programs & Events
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/wp-content/uploads/After-the-Island-Programming-Thumbnail.png
GEO:43.6679938;-79.3935128
