BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95d6d21c197d11325c2d52"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Reaping What She Sows: Women at the Forefront of Toronto Food Syst
 ems Change
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261010T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261010T163000
UID:6a95d6d21c197d11325c2d52@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Global events - from pandemics\, war\, and foodborne illnesses
  - have threatened farmers' livelihoods\, created supermarket shortages\, 
 and revealed to consumers that our food system is broken. In the face of t
 hese threats to our well-being and food security\, what can we do as a co
 mmunity to strengthen our local food system?\nMeet\nNancy Matsumoto\n\, au
 thor of\nReaping What She Sows\n: How Women Are Rebuilding Our Broken Food
  System\n\, who will introduce her book and highlight Toronto women chang
 emakers building up local and regional supply chains to combat the destruc
 tive effects of Big Food.\nPaul DeCampo\n\, representative of Karma Co-op 
 Grocery Store will moderate the discussion\, which will include the follow
 ing guest panelists:\nJenn Pfenning\nfrom Pfennings Organic Farm\nNurith 
 Jungreis\nfrom Sunflower Kitchen\nSabrina Banh\nfrom Fisherfolk\nTogether
 \, they will discuss the importance of a resilient\, transparent\, climat
 e-friendly local and regional food system—for our health\, the health of
  workers\, animals and communities\, and the future of our planet. Attende
 es will be inspired to learn how they can also help create positive food s
 ystem change and contribute to our regional food security.\nPresented in c
 elebration of Women's History Month\, as part of Small Business Month\, a
 nd as part of TPL's environmental program series\,\nOur Fragile Planet\n\,
  supported by Friends of Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.\nFree pr
 ogram. Please register. The presentation will include an audience Q&A.\nP
 lace a hold or borrow from Toronto Public Library:\nReaping What She Sows\
 n: How Women Are Rebuilding Our Broken Food System\n(book)\nReaping What S
 he Sows\nReaping What She Sows\nReaping What She Sows\n(ebook)\nBio\nNancy
  Matsumoto\n\, an award-winning freelance writer and editor who writes abo
 ut agroecology\, food sovereignty\, food\, drink\, and Japanese American c
 ulture and history.\nPaul DeCampo\nhas worked as an educator\, food resear
 cher and wine marketer. A Karma Co-op Grocery Store member since 1997\, P
 aul has served in various volunteer roles at this community-owned\, co-ope
 rative grocery store\, including on the Board of Directors and as General 
 Manager.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this
  form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Librarie
 s Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be u
 sed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Question
 s about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answer
 line staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Tor
 onto ON M4W 2G8.\nReaping What She Sows
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95d6d21c197d11325c2d52
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
