BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Civic Tech Toronto": "https://guild.host/events/civic-meetup-564-a-datadriven-w2fxru"}
X-SOURCE-ID:guildhost_civic_tech
SUMMARY:Civic Meetup #564: A Data-Driven Approach to Toronto's Electoral G
 eography
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T210000
UID:abce6f82a43b485ffeeb262649402740@guild.host
DESCRIPTION:Venue confirmed: Health Sciences Building (University of Toron
 to)\, Room 108\, 155 College St. Please note: this is not Myhal Centre as 
 originally listed.&#x20\;\n\nTopic: A Data-Driven Approach to Toronto's El
 ectoral Geography\n\nWith colleagues\, Zach Taylor built for research purp
 oses fine-grained maps of historical election results in Toronto and other
  cities. He will discuss how they built the maps\, what they have used the
 m for\, and how spatial data like this could be used in the future.\n\nSpe
 aker: Zack Taylor (he/him)\n\nZack Taylor is a professor of Political Scie
 nce and Geography at the University of Western Ontario where he specialize
 s in urban politics\, local governance\, and political geography.\n\nAgend
 a:\n\n7:00-7:20 Welcome and Introductions\n\n7:20-7:50 Presentation and Q\
 \&A/discussion\n\n7:50-9:00 Breakout groups\n\n‎\n\nCode of Conduct: htt
 ps://civictech.ca/about-us\n\n‎\n\nCheck in with us on the Civic Tech To
 ronto Slack: https://link.civictech.ca/chat\n\n‎\n\nAbout Us:\n\nOur wee
 kly civic meetups bring together Torontonians (designers\, coders\, urban 
 planners\, government staff\, mappers\, policy-makers\, students\, communi
 cations strategists\, community organizers\, and more) who share an intere
 st in making Toronto more responsive\, prosperous\, sustainable\, and equi
 table through design\, tech\, and data.\n\nCome and be part of it!\n\nFor 
 more info: https://civictech.ca\n\n\\--\n\nSuggest a Speaker:&#x20\;\n\nIn
 terested in speaking at a future event\, or know someone who'd be a great 
 speaker? We'd love to hear from you: https://link.civictech.ca/speaker-sug
 gestions\n\nSuggest a Venue:&#x20\;\n\nHave a space that could host up to 
 80 civic-minded folks for an evening? Consider recommending your venue: ht
 tps://link.civictech.ca/venue-suggestions\n
LOCATION:Online: https://link.civictech.ca/zoom / Health Sciences Building
URL:https://guild.host/events/civic-meetup-564-a-datadriven-w2fxru
X-SOURCE:Civic Tech Toronto
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Luma Toronto Discover": "https://luma.com/tf6s6ycw"}
X-SOURCE-ID:luma_toronto_discover
SUMMARY:Cutting The Fashion Body: Why The Fashion Image Is No Longer Still
  by Nathalie Khan
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T210000
UID:evt-lsCH62VBZHvkgX6@events.lu.ma
DESCRIPTION:Address:\nBeyond Space\nToronto\, ON\nCanada\n\nAn extra Octob
 er meeting\, bringing Fashion Book Club to the east end!\nWe'll be gatheri
 ng at La Maison Atelier\, the concept store of Toronto label Beyond Space\
 , to read Nathalie Khan's "Cutting the Fashion Body: Why the Fashion Image
  Is No Longer Still."\nExploring how fashion film has changed the way we s
 ee clothes and the body\, Khan's essay asks what means for fashion to exis
 t in motion. We'll screen three of the four fashion films Khan references\
 , and discuss how moving images shape the way we see and make fashion toda
 y.\nSee you there!\nPDF here\n\nHosted by Fashion Book Club & La Maison At
 elier & Cafe
LOCATION:Beyond Space\, 349 Jones Ave\, Toronto\, ON M4J 3G4\, Canada
URL:https://luma.com/tf6s6ycw
X-SOURCE:Luma Toronto Discover
END:VEVENT
