SUMMARY:Taming the Tyrant: Democracy and Political Accountability in Athen
 s and Today
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T190000
UID:6ab7dafe94b74515467b93ca@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:How much power should a single individual have in a democracy?
  And how might ordinary citizens hold political elites to account? Dr. Ell
 a Street (PhD\, Political Theory\, University of Toronto) takes us back to
  classical Athens to explore these enduring questions. Examining the remar
 kable institutions and cultural practices Athenians developed to keep thei
 r leaders in check\, she considers what it really means for the demos—th
 e people—to rule supreme.\nDespite all that separates ancient and modern
  democracy\, Athens offers surprising lessons for our own moment. As democ
 racies today grapple with powerful individuals—from mayors and heads of 
 state to corporate and technology elites—what might we learn from the At
 henians about keeping power in the hands of the people? Register at tpl.ca
  or by calling 416-396-8943\nToronto's municipal election takes place on O
 ctober 26\, 2026. Check out the\nKnowYourVote.TO\nwebsite and the\nCelebra
 te Democracy\npage to learn more about the upcoming election\, access impo
 rtant voter resources\, and discover activities and interactive library pr
 ograms for all ages happening at a branch near you.\nThis initiative is ma
 de possible by donations to the Toronto Public Library Foundation and is g
 enerously supported by the McConnell Foundation.
LOCATION:Agincourt\, Multipurpose Room\, 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ab7dafe94b74515467b93ca
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
