X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardinermuseum": "https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/from-ancient-americas-to-ancestral-abiayala/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardinermuseum
X-SOURCE:Gardiner Museum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T200000
DTSTAMP:20260914T204212Z
CREATED:20260914T201341Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260914T204212Z
UID:147219-1792692000-1792699200@www.gardinermuseum.on.ca
SUMMARY:From Ancient Americas to Ancestral Abiayala
DESCRIPTION:From Ancient Americas to Ancestral Abiayala            \n                                    \n                                \n                        \n                                                \n                                                        Get Tickets                        \n                                            \n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n			\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Thursday October 22\, 2026\n6:00 pm – 8:00 pm\n \nThe Diane Wolfe Lecture \nSpeaker: Juliana Ramírez Herrera\, Ph.D.\, Assistant Professor of Indigenous Art and Archaeology of the Ancestral Americas and of Colonial Latin American Art\, University of California\, Berkeley \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Join Dr. Juliana Ramírez Herrera\, curator of the Gardiner Museum’s Ancestral Abiayala Gallery—formerly Ancient Americas—for a conversation on repatriation\, broadly understood\, and how it shaped her vision for the renovated space. \nDrawing on other threads of her research\, Dr. Ramírez Herrera will situate Ancestral Abiayala within larger conversations about the mobility of belongings\, violent and otherwise\, and what it might mean to care for them responsibly in museum practice and scholarly writing. \nThe talk offers a behind-the-scenes look at what went into developing the Ancestral Abiayala Gallery\, connecting its questions to broader intellectual debates in the field. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n			\n\n        \n          \n          \n                        \n               \n                General : $25\n                                        \n                        \n               \n                Gardiner Friends : $21\n                                        \n                        \n               \n                Patron Members : Free\n                                        \n                        \n               \n                Students : Free\n                                        \n                      \n\n           \n            \n              Gardiner Friends\, log in to access your discount.* \nNot a Gardiner Friend yet? Join now at the Friend level or above to save . \n*Discounts on programs and classes do not apply to ‘Annual Pass’ level. \n            \n                    \n          \n            \n  \n            \n                                Get Tickets\n              \n                        \n           \n                \n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                \n          \n          \n        \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    About the Speaker\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Dr. Juliana Ramírez Herrera\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Dr. Juliana Ramírez Herrera is Assistant Professor of Indigenous Art and Archaeology of the Ancestral Americas and of Colonial Latin American Art at the University of California\, Berkeley. She specializes in the so-called small-scale societies of pre-Conquest and early colonial Panama\, Colombia\, the Caribbean\, and Amazonia. Before joining UC Berkeley\, she was a SSHRC Postdoctoral Fellow at the University of Toronto and the Gardiner Museum of Ceramic Art\, where she collaborated with curators Dr. Sequoia Miller\, Dr. Karine Tsoumis\, and Franchesca Herbert-Spence on the renovation of the Museum’s ground floor. She earned her PhD from Harvard University and her MA from the University of Toronto. \nPhoto: Julie Wolf \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/from-ancient-americas-to-ancestral-abiayala/
LOCATION:Gardiner Museum\, 111 Queen's Park\, Toronto\, ON\, M5S 2C7\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Programs & Events
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/wp-content/uploads/Wolfe-Lecture-2026.jpg
GEO:43.6679938;-79.3935128
X-APPLE-STRUCTURED-LOCATION;VALUE=URI;X-ADDRESS=Gardiner Museum 111 Queen's Park Toronto ON M5S 2C7 Canada;X-APPLE-RADIUS=500;X-TITLE=111 Queen's Park:geo:-79.3935128,43.6679938
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Tool Library": "https://ttlmarket.com/products/cnc-milling-for-builders-oct-22-thursdays-6-week-comprehensive"}
X-SOURCE-ID:toronto_tool_library
SUMMARY:CNC Milling for Builders - Oct 22  [Thursdays - 6 Week Comprehensi
 ve]
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T210000
UID:ttl-11093097840833@ttlmarket.com
DESCRIPTION:CNC for Builders \n\n \n \n\n \n In this course at TTLMakerspa
 ce\, you will go from beginner to a practical working knowledge of CAD mod
 elling and CNC machining with an automated milling machine\, specifically 
 for furniture\, cabinetry\, frames etc. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Starting with
  desktop versions of CNC machines\, you’ll work your way through lessons
  up to the industrial Omni 1350 6’ x 8’ CNC milling machine. \n \n \n\
 n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Schedule : \n \n \n Six three-hour weekly 
 classes\, Thursdays 6pm to 9pm. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Outcomes : \n \n \n A
 t the end of this course you will have achieved the following goals: \n \n
  \n- \n A complete understanding of the steps needed to go from concept to
  completed part \n\n- \n Familiarity with the properties of a variety of h
 ardwood materials \n\n- \n Experience and comprehension of the most import
 ant principles of CAD modelling \n\n- \n Selecting and programming the mos
 t commonly used tools \n\n- \n Full understanding of the most common machi
 ning operations \n\n- \n Experience creating tool paths with CAM software 
 \n\n- \n Setting up a CNC mill and performing operations \n\n- \n Machine 
 maintenance and Safety \n\n \n \n Following this course\, you’ll be cert
 ified to use desktop mills\, and book time with the industrial mill at TTL
 Makerspace (with your membership). You will also be able to join the growi
 ng community of TTL Makers sharing CNC knowledge on our Discord community.
  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Useful for: furniture\, cabinets\, flat-pack\, deskt
 ops\, boats\, frames \n \n \n\n \n \n Want to get notified next time class
 es are posted? \n\n Join our mailing list!
LOCATION:TTLMakerspace\, 192 Spadina Ave.\, Toronto\, ON
URL:https://ttlmarket.com/products/cnc-milling-for-builders-oct-22-thursda
 ys-6-week-comprehensive
X-SOURCE:Toronto Tool Library
END:VEVENT
