SUMMARY:"Make It Heard 3": Civic Problem-Solving & Digital Advocacy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T200000
UID:6abec9f911bbf300684ab1db@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Every community has problems. This series gives you the tools 
 to do something about them. Over three sessions you will define a real civ
 ic challenge in your neighbourhood\, design a creative response\, and buil
 d something: a digital campaign\, a poster\, a video pitch\, or even a 3D-
 printed prototype that communicates your idea to the public. Come ready to
  think\, build\, and be heard.\nThis program is part of TPL's Innovator in
  Residence series focused on civic innovation. From October 5 to November 
 28\, join programs\, workshops and one-on-one consultations led by technol
 ogy strategist Ismail Mohamud to learn how technology can help you create 
 positive change in your community. Learn more at\ntpl.ca/iir\n.\nInformati
 on Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected 
 by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20
 (a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and p
 rogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the lib
 rary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nansw
 erline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Second Floor\, Digital Innovation Hub
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6abec9f911bbf300684ab1db
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Teens": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa082354cb69d003e26ac5e"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_teens
SUMMARY:Why We Misunderstand the Middle East and Why it Matters
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T200000
UID:6aa082354cb69d003e26ac5e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Participants will gain a clearer\, more grounded understanding
  of how common misconceptions about the Middle East are formed and why the
 y persist. They will learn to recognize how media narratives\, language\, 
 and framing shape interpretation\, often in subtle ways.\nThe session will
  offer practical tools for questioning familiar assumptions\, evaluating i
 nformation more critically\, and approaching discussions about the region 
 with greater nuance and context. Participants will also gain a broader app
 reciation of the historical\, cultural\, linguistic\, and political divers
 ity that exists within the Middle East\, helping them move beyond simplifi
 ed understandings.\nPresented by Dr. Hamid Yazdi
LOCATION:St. Lawrence\, Ground Floor\, South Side
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa082354cb69d003e26ac5e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
