BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Young Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51a33b6c71003e5de7bb"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_young_adults
SUMMARY:Run Your One-Person Business with AI
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T200000
UID:6a9b51a33b6c71003e5de7bb@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:When you're the whole company\, the admin never ends: emails\,
  scheduling\, proposals\, follow-ups. What if you could design a system to
  handle the busywork?\nIn this class\, you'll learn to design an administr
 ative system for a one-person business with AI as your assistant: intake p
 ipelines\, email drafting\, client communication\, and scheduling. Learn t
 o map work into four piles: what's rules-based\, what AI drafts\, what nee
 ds your judgment\, and what should never be automated. You'll build reusab
 le templates and leave with a clearer plan for how AI could help your busi
 ness.\nPrerequisites: Mouse\, keyboarding and internet skills. Computers
  are provided. No coding experience required.\nRegistration is required a
 nd will start October 9\, 2026 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be g
 uaranteed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nAfter this time\, 
 remaining seats will be filled on a first come\, first serve basis until c
 apacity is reached. If you wish to save your work from this hands-on prog
 ram\, please bring your own USB stick.\nAbout the AI Tools Used in This Pr
 ogram\nThis program includes activities that use agentic AI tools (listed 
 below) on library laptops.\nYou do not need to create your own account for
  this program.\nParticipants are encouraged to follow the Resident’s gui
 dance throughout sessions\, which include best practices on using AI.\nWe 
 encourage you to review the privacy statements of each tool prior to atten
 ding this program:\nClaude Code\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy
  policy\nClaude Cowork\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\n
 Google Anti-Gravity\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nGoo
 gle AI Studio\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nInformati
 on Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected 
 by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20
 (a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and p
 rogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the lib
 rary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nansw
 erline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51a33b6c71003e5de7bb
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8dad66aafa6100295ef816"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Changing the Narrative: Toronto’s Indigenous – Settler History
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T200000
UID:6a8dad66aafa6100295ef816@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn more about Toronto's multifaceted Indigenous history\, w
 hich has often been ignored or minimized due to the telling of the region'
 s history only from settler perspectives. Join us for a panel discussion a
 bout a public history project that worked with five local First Nations to
  create a more accurate and inclusive story of the region's past\, and is
  transforming the way settlement of 19th century Ontario is represented a
 t The Village at Black Creek (formerly Black Creek Pioneer Village).\nChan
 ging the Narrative: Reconnecting Settler and Indigenous Histories at Black
  Creek Pioneer Village was a collaborative project of York University hist
 orians\, The Village at Black Creek\, Jumblies Theatre\, and the Mississau
 gas of the Credit\, Chippewas of Rama\, Six Nations of the Grand River\, C
 hippewas of Georgina Island\, and Mississaugas of Scugog Island First Nati
 ons.\nThis panel discussion will share research highlights\, reflect on th
 e process of collaboration\, and explore how the project is expanding and 
 enriching storytelling at one of the region’s prime living history museu
 ms.\nThe project’s findings have also been published in\nWhere Histories
  Meet: Indigenous and Settler Encounters in the Toronto Area\n\, by Victor
 ia Freeman. The book explores this history from multiple perspectives in a
 n accessible and richly illustrated format and includes excerpts from inte
 rviews with twenty Indigenous elders and knowledge keepers. It has been no
 minated for a\n2026 City of Toronto Book Award\n.\nPanel members include:\
 nVictoria      Freeman\n\, historical researcher and author of\nWhere Hist
 ories Meet:      Indigenous and Settler Encounters in the Toronto Area\n.\
 nJennifer      Bonnell\n\, professor in the Department of History at York 
 University and the      lead of the Changing the Narrative Project.\nAllis
 on      White\n\, curator at The Village at Black Creek (Toronto and Regio
 n      Conservation Authority).\nDarin      Wybenga\n\, heritage interpret
 er for the Mississaugas of the Credit First      Nation\, and member of th
 e Research Committee for the Changing the      Narrative project.\nLauri  
     Hoeg\n\, Anishinaabe artist and cultural advisor from the Chippewas of
       Georgina Island First Nation\, and member of the Research Committee 
 for the      Changing the Narrative project.\nThe panel discussion will la
 st approximately 60 minutes\, followed by a Q&A with audience members. No 
 registration required.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, 1st Floor\, Beeton Hall
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8dad66aafa6100295ef816
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
