BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardinermuseum": "https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/adaptive-ceramic-histories/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardinermuseum
X-SOURCE:Gardiner Museum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270329T235959
DTSTAMP:20260901T203045Z
CREATED:20260901T203044Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260901T203045Z
UID:147050-1795132800-1806364799@www.gardinermuseum.on.ca
SUMMARY:Adaptive Ceramic Histories
DESCRIPTION:Adaptive Ceramic Histories                                                                          \n        \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n	\n\n	\n\n\n			\nEvents\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    \n\nNovember 20\, 2026 – March 29\, 2027\nThe Hilary and Galen Weston Foundation Hall. Ground Floor\nIncluded with admission. Free for Gardiner Members.\n\n\n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n  Get Tickets\n\n\n\n  Become a Member\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Curated by Alexandra Box\, Gardiner Museum Curatorial Resident \nEngage in an exploration of materially mediated dynamics of care and adaption. Adaptive Ceramic Histories presents an expansive way of looking at histories of medical and adaptive ceramics created for sick or disabled users. The exhibition invites you to delve into the design of care from the vantage point of users\, departing from traditional histories of medicine centering on medical professionals. Instead\, it considers the possibilities of pleasure and metaphor to build a more fulsome knowledge of patient care to craft the future. \nAdaptive Ceramic Histories features objects from the Gardiner Museum’s permanent collection alongside loans to highlight the importance of ceramic materials at different moments in the history of Western medicine from the 17th century to the present day. \nThe Gardiner Museum Curatorial Residency is made possible through the generous support of the Rebanks Family. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    About the Curator\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Alexandra Box\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Alexandra Box is an arts professional and artist. She has been awarded fellowships and artist residencies through Medalta Potteries\, Salt Spring Arts Council\, and Point Gallery. Alexandra is a curatorial participant in the Knowledge Incubator & Mentorship Initiative through Redefine Arts. Her writing has been published by Hearth\, Peripheral Review\, and The Capilano Review. Alexandra serves on the Board at Forest City Gallery and is a contributor to the Media Collaboratory. She holds a BFA (2020) in ceramics and curatorial practices from Emily Carr University and an MA (2024) in Art History from the University of Toronto. \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Header image: Feeding cup\, Rouen\, France\, early 1700s\, Tin-glazed earthenware\, The Pierre Karch and Mariel O’Neill-Karch Collection\, G14.4.20 \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/adaptive-ceramic-histories/
LOCATION:Gardiner Museum\, 111 Queen's Park\, Toronto\, ON\, M5S 2C7\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/wp-content/uploads/Adaptive-Ceramic-Histories-thumbnail.jpg
GEO:43.6679938;-79.3935128
X-APPLE-STRUCTURED-LOCATION;VALUE=URI;X-ADDRESS=Gardiner Museum 111 Queen's Park Toronto ON M5S 2C7 Canada;X-APPLE-RADIUS=500;X-TITLE=111 Queen's Park:geo:-79.3935128,43.6679938
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Philosophy": "https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/eastern-study-group-of-the-north-american-kant-society/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:philosophy
X-SOURCE:UofT Philosophy
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261120
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261122
DTSTAMP:20260929T144138Z
CREATED:20260910T170028Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260929T144138Z
UID:35761-1795132800-1795305599@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Eastern Study Group of the North American Kant Society
DESCRIPTION:Join us for the two-day meeting of the North American Kant Society Eastern Study Group. \nDAY 1: Friday\, November 20 – Room 100  \n\n\n\n9:30 AM – 10:30 AM\nPractical Philosophy\n“Kant’s Purely Practical Concept of Freedom”\nNoa Buckle (Toronto)\n*Nominated for Markus Herz Award*\n\n\n10:40 AM – 12:10 PM\nPractical Philosophy\n“Is there a Right to Visit? Juridical Community\, Spatial Fragmentation and Communication in Towards Perpetual Peace”\nAlice Pinheiro Walla (McMaster)\n\n\n 12:10 PM – 2:00 PM\nLunch Break\n\n\n2:00 PM – 3:30 PM\nMetaphysics\n“Kant’s Possibility Proof and the Myth of the ‘Metaphysical Kant'”\nMaya Krishnan (Chicago)\n\n\n3:40 PM – 5:10 PM\nMetaphysics\n“Analogical Grounding: Kant on the Relation between Phenomena and Noumena”\nLorenzo Spagnesi (Trier)\n\n\n5:30 Pm – 7:30 PM\n Keynote Address\n“The Juridical Interpretation of the Transcendental Deduction”\nArthur Ripstein (Toronto)\n\n\n\nDAY 2: Saturday\, November 21 – Room 100  \n\n\n\n9:30 AM – 10:30 AM\nThe Possibility of Cognition\n“Schematism\, Homogeneity\, and a Third Thing”\nFearghus Horan (Johns Hopkins University)\n*Nominated for Markus Herz Award and winner of the graduate travel stipend \n\n\n10:40 AM – 12:10 PM\nThe Possibility of Cognition\n“Humility and Relationality”\nDai Heide (Simon Fraser)\n\n\n 12:10 PM – 2:00 PM\nLunch Break\n\n\n2:00 PM – 3:30 PM\nJudgement\n“The Dilemma of Existential Judgments”\nMario Schärli (HU-Berlin)\n\n\n3:40 PM – 5:10 PM\nJudgement\n“Kant on Truth”\nAlexandra Newton (UC-Riverside)\n\n\n5:30 Pm – 7:30 PM\n Keynote Address\n“Kant and Schopenhauer on Freedom of the Will”\nAnja Jauernig (NYU)\n\n\n\nDownload the schedule.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/eastern-study-group-of-the-north-american-kant-society/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building 100
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ORGANIZER;CN="Nick Stang":MAILTO:nick.stang@utoronto.ca
END:VEVENT
