X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Fact or Fiction: Identifying and Combatting Misinformation and Di
 sinformation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T153000
UID:6aad90be65abe8002a7a5c79@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you ready to separate fact from fiction in a world filled 
 with misinformation and disinformation? Join us for an interactive and ent
 ertaining program where we'll dive into the fascinating world of misinform
 ation\, exploring its origins\, impact\, and how to combat it effectively.
 \nThis program is perfect for curious minds of all ages who want to arm th
 emselves with the skills needed to navigate the complex landscape of onlin
 e information responsibly and safely.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe
  personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Libr
 ary under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). T
 he information will only be used for event and program registration\, deli
 very and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your informat
 ion can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-
 5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:S. Walter Stewart\, Learning Centre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aad90be65abe8002a7a5c79
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Older Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3963a8dcc1022f003a66e2"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Birds and Our Christmas Traditions
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T150000
UID:6a3963a8dcc1022f003a66e2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Birds are woven into the fabric of Christmas traditions\, serv
 ing as powerful symbols of hope\, renewal\, and warmth during the winter m
 onths. Their presence during the holidays\, whether through festive orname
 nts\, songs\, or sightings at feeders\, recalls ancient lore that connects
  birds with messages of love and positivity. By inviting birds into our li
 ves and honoring them through tradition\, we not only support these treasu
 red visitors but also embrace the spirit of Christmas—one of generosity\
 , harmony\, and the celebration of nature’s enduring beauty. Jim Mackie
 wicz is the owner of Wild Birds Unlimited and has been helping customers w
 ith backyard bird feeding and connecting to nature for 25 years.\nInformat
 ion Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected
  by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 2
 0(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and 
 program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the li
 brary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nans
 werline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Pleasant\, Basement program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3963a8dcc1022f003a66e2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 School Age": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a88d0d357c264087ad2d3a3"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_school_age
SUMMARY:Artists in the Library:  Milkweed Ornament
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T160000
UID:6a88d0d357c264087ad2d3a3@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Part 4 of a series of workshops for children and adults\, expl
 oring local flora through embroidery and felt collage. This event is part
  of the\nArtists in the Library\nprogram\, hosted by Artist\, Sarah Zanche
 tta.\nAll ages\nprogram\; children ages 6 to 14 must be accompanied by a p
 arent or caregiver.  Registration is required. Space is limited.\nNote:\nE
 ach workshop requires separate registration. Registered participant spaces
  are held for 15 minutes after which drop-ins are allowed\nMeet the Reside
 nt:\nSarah Zanchetta (she/they) is a queer textile artist and educator who
 se practice dives into the loss of connection and knowledge of poisonous p
 lants in North America. Zanchetta uses textiles\, texts\, and hosts commun
 ity workshops to create intimate works and spaces where we can unfold our 
 curiosities\, care and anxieties about what grows beside our feet. She ho
 lds a Master of Visual Studies from the University of Toronto (2023) and a
  BFA from OCAD University (2018).\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe per
 sonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library 
 under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The i
 nformation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery
  and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information 
 can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981
 \, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Dennis\, Lower Level\, Room 2
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a88d0d357c264087ad2d3a3
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — School Age
END:VEVENT
